course: world
lesson: 100
title: Communication: letters, phones, radio and the internet
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: For most of history a long message could travel only as fast as the person or animal carrying it. A letter from Britain to Australia went by ship and took months; an answer took months more. People also used signals that travel by sight or sound — beacon fires on hilltops, smoke and drums — but these could carry only simple messages agreed in advance.
explain: In 1840 Britain began the Penny Black, the first postage stamp: the sender paid a penny in advance, whatever the distance, and sending letters became cheap for everyone.
explain: The electric telegraph changed everything. A message became pulses of electric current in a wire, written in Morse code as short and long signals — dots and dashes. From 1866 a cable under the Atlantic Ocean carried messages between Europe and America in minutes instead of weeks.
explain: In 1876 Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone, which turns the voice into a changing electric current and back into sound at the other end. Radio needs no wire at all: around 1900 Guglielmo Marconi sent messages with radio waves, which travel at the speed of light, and in 1901 he sent a signal across the Atlantic.
explain: Today phones, radio, television and the internet all work by the same big idea: turn a message into a signal, send the signal very fast, and turn it back into a message at the other end. A broadcast, like radio or TV, goes from one sender to many listeners; a phone call goes from one person to another.
fact: Before the telegraph, a long message could travel only as fast as a person, horse or ship could carry it.
fact: The Penny Black of 1840 in Britain was the first postage stamp you stick on a letter.
fact: The electric telegraph sent messages as pulses of electric current along wires.
fact: Morse code writes each letter as a pattern of short and long signals, called dots and dashes.
fact: In Morse code the distress signal SOS is three dots, three dashes, three dots.
fact: From 1866 a telegraph cable under the Atlantic Ocean joined Europe and North America.
fact: Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876, which means he was given the official right to it as his invention.
fact: A telephone turns sound into a changing electric current and turns it back into sound at the other end.
fact: Radio waves travel through the air and empty space at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres every second.
fact: In 1901 Guglielmo Marconi sent a radio signal across the Atlantic Ocean.
fact: A broadcast, such as radio or television, sends the same message from one sender to many people at once.
fact: Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web in 1989.
exercise: Before the telegraph, what limited how fast a letter could travel? | The speed of the person, horse or ship carrying it | The number of words written in the letter | The colour of the paper it was written on | How loudly the sender read it aloud
exercise: What was the Penny Black? | The first stick-on postage stamp | The first coin used in Britain | The first telephone in London | The first newspaper ever printed
exercise: Why did the Penny Black make letters cheap? | The sender paid one penny in advance, whatever the distance | Letters were carried free by the army | Stamps were paid for by the king himself | Letters went by telegraph instead of by hand
exercise: How did the electric telegraph send messages? | As pulses of electric current along wires | As sounds shouted loudly from tower to tower | As letters carried by fast trains | As light shone through glass fibres
exercise: What is Morse code? | Letters written as short and long signals | A secret language spoken only by old sailors | A way of writing with only numbers | A set of flags that show the weather
exercise: What is SOS in Morse code? | Three dots, three dashes, three dots | Three dashes, three dots, three dashes | One dot, one dash, one dot | Six dots in a row
exercise: What did the Atlantic telegraph cable of 1866 change? | Messages crossed the ocean in minutes, not weeks | Ships could cross the ocean in one day | People could talk to each other by video across the sea | Letters no longer needed any stamps
exercise: Who patented the telephone in 1876? | Alexander Graham Bell | Guglielmo Marconi of Italy | Samuel Morse of America | Thomas Edison of New Jersey
exercise: What does a telephone do with the sound of your voice? | Turns it into an electric current and back into sound | Sends the air from your mouth down a long tube | Records it on paper and then posts it on to the listener | Makes it louder so it can be heard far away
exercise: What does radio need instead of wires? | Radio waves through the air | A long tube filled with water | Bright light from the Sun by day | A long line of fast messengers
exercise: How fast do radio waves travel? | At the speed of light | At the speed of sound | As fast as a jet plane | As fast as a train
exercise: What did Marconi do in 1901? | Sent a radio signal across the Atlantic | Made the very first telephone call in history | Invented the first stick-on postage stamp | Laid the first undersea telegraph cable to America
exercise: What is a broadcast? | One sender sending to many people at once | Two people talking privately on the telephone | A single letter sent to one address by post | A short message written on the back of a stamp
exercise: Who invented the World Wide Web? | Tim Berners-Lee | Alexander Graham Bell | Samuel Morse | Guglielmo Marconi
exercise: What big idea do phones, radio and the internet share? | A message is turned into a signal and back again | Every message must be carried by a person | Messages travel only during the daytime | Signals can only ever travel through long metal wires
test: In 1850 a merchant in London wants a reply from a friend in Sydney. Why will he wait so long? | The letter and the reply must both go by ship | Letters to Australia had to be written twice | The telegraph to Australia was much too expensive to use | Post offices opened only once a year then
test: Beacon fires on hilltops could warn a whole country of an invasion in hours. Why could they not send a long story? | A fire can only show a simple signal agreed in advance | Fire travels more slowly than a horse | Beacons could only be lit in the daytime | Nobody could see fire from another hill
test: A ship sends three short, three long and three short flashes of light at night. What is it telling you? | It is in danger and needs help | It is about to leave the harbour | It has just caught many fish | It is greeting another ship
test: A radio signal is sent to astronauts on the Moon, about 384,000 kilometres away. Roughly how long does it take to arrive? | A little more than one second | About one hour | About one day | It arrives at exactly the same instant
test: Lightning and thunder happen together, but you see the flash before you hear the thunder. What does this show about radio waves, which travel like light? | They are much faster than sound | They are slower than sound | They travel at the speed of sound | They cannot travel through air
test: Which is a broadcast rather than a one-to-one message? | A radio news programme | A private phone call to Gran | A letter to your aunt in Wales | A text message to one friend
test: A storm snaps a telegraph wire between two towns. Which way of sending messages still works between them? | Radio, because it needs no wire | The telegraph, by shouting louder | A telephone on the same wire | Morse code sent along the broken wire
test: Which list puts these in the order they appeared? | Postage stamp, telephone, radio across the Atlantic, World Wide Web | Telephone, postage stamp, World Wide Web, radio | World Wide Web, radio, telephone, postage stamp | Radio, World Wide Web, postage stamp, telephone
test: Why could the Morse telegraph send any word, unlike beacon fires? | Every letter had its own pattern of signals | The wires carried the sender's real voice | The operators knew every message in advance | Each word had its own separate wire
test: A message travels around the Earth's equator, about 40,000 kilometres, at the speed of light. About how long would it take? | A small part of a second | Roughly ten whole seconds | About one minute and a half | Around one hour, maybe more
test: whats SOS in morse | Three dots, three dashes, three dots | Three dashes, three dots, three dashes | One dot, one dash, one dot | Six dots and six dashes
test: how'd people send a long msg before the telegraph | Someone had to carry it by foot, horse or ship | They just rang them up on the telephone from home | They sent it by radio | They emailed it
test: Leo says Tim Berners-Lee invented the phone. true? | No, he invented the World Wide Web; Bell patented the telephone | Yes, he made the first phone in 1876 | Yes, and also the radio | No, he invented the telegraph
test: my brother reckons radio waves are way slower than sound. right? | No, they travel at the speed of light, much faster than sound | Yes, radio waves move slower than sound | Yes, they move as fast as a car | No, they travel at the same speed as sound
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Morse code? | Each letter is written as a pattern of short and long signals | Each letter is written as a coloured flag | Each letter is spoken aloud over a telephone | Each letter is drawn as a small picture
exam: Identify the person who patented the telephone, together with the year thereof. | Alexander Graham Bell, in 1876 | Guglielmo Marconi, in 1901 | Tim Berners-Lee, in 1989 | Alexander Graham Bell, in 1840
exam: Ascertain the speed at which radio waves travel. | The speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres per second | The speed of sound, about one kilometre in three seconds | The speed of a fast train | About 300 kilometres per second
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a broadcast. | One sender transmits the same message to many people at once | One sender transmits a private message to one person | Many senders transmit to one person | Messages are carried by hand from house to house
